the DataStore+InternalDataStore interface pattern tends to be leaky, and it exposes details about the implementation in the public interface. I am proposing an alternative design where only a simple method is exposed:

{code}
interface DataProcessor<T> {
    void process(Message<T> m)
}
{code}

a public hierarchy of messages is provided:

- Insert<T>
- Update<T>
- Delete<T>

end users are not supposed to interact with these messages directly, unless they want to write their own DataProcessor. In this case, they can easily choose which message they want to listen to, and discard the others e.g.:

{code}
DataProcessor<T> p = m -> { 
  if (m instanceof Insert) { /* do something */ }
  // ignore everything else
}
{code}

at the same time, this allows us to define internal messages, traveling on the same "logic" bus; e.g. we can provide a public implementation for Update<T> that any Processor can use, but also extend it to an internal EntryPointUpdate<T> that is only recognized by EntryPointProcessor instances (and processed by any other DataProcessor as a regular Update otherwise)

upsides: 
  - our interface is cleaner
downsides: 
  - we are now using instanceof instead of method dispatch (minor problem probably)
  - testing harder ? not sure...

